A||N||2||merlin||Painting rocker panels||||1||
Z||000000||merlin||08-08-2000||12:29 PM||||I own an 87GT and I was considering painting the rocker panels to match the color of the body, similar to the 88 model year.  I was quoted a $300 price tag to have this done by a paint shop…  OUCH!   Has anyone had success with using only spray cans to paint the rocker panels?  How durable is the paint, since it would be more prone to chips given the location of the rocker panels?  Is it easier to paint the rocker panels on the car or should they be removed to avoid breaking those clips I keep hearing about?  <P>Thanks for any help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||08-08-2000||07:26 PM||||For the rocker panels to look good, paint them off the body to avoid overspray on everything else.  If you can find spray paint in a can to match your body color, practice painting.  And yes some of the plastic pop rivets that hold them on will get broken during removal.<P>I've had mine done twice since I've owned the car, but I stayed with the stock grey color at Mr. Goodwrench.  I think the last time it was $140. But again I didn't try to change the color.  You'll have the nose and rear facsia to do. More work.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||PJ||08-08-2000||08:37 PM||||Just a little warning from personal experience. The rocker panels are made of TPO (Thermal Poly Olefin??) and need a special primer to make the paint stick. If you're painting over paint, no problem. If you've got bare plastic showing you've got to strip and repaint the whole piece. The primer melts paint and is impossible to blend.<P>PJ<BR>ITA #66 Black<BR> ||||reg||1
